    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d407">

                <pb facs="0503" n="421" sameAs="frus:frus1865p3/pg421"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Bigelow</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 293.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1865-10-30"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >October</hi> 30, 1865.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I have your despatch of October 6, <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">No.</hi> 180. While you have very properly given us
                    the inferences which you drew from the conversation of Mr. Drouyn de Lhuys,
                    which was elicited by the reading of my despatch of September 6, you have, at
                    the same time, implied that he would probably return to the subject at a later
                    day. The President waits with very considerable interest for the views of the
                    French government. Whatever has occurred in connexion with the subject since my
                    despatch was written has tended to enhance that interest.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Bigelow,</hi> Esq., <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
